{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "סמך אל קנא ללא תשא", :translit "samakh el kanai le-lo tisa", :gloss "He juxtaposed 'Jealous' to 'You shall not take'"} {:he "שכל הנשבע לשקר כאילו עובד עכו\"ם", :translit "she-kol ha-nishba la-sheker ke-ilu oved akum", :gloss "that everyone who swears falsely is as if he worships idolatry"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (סמיכות) of adjacent phrases 'Jealous' and 'You shall not take'"}], :frame {:text "The principle of semikhut ha-parshiyot — that adjacent biblical phrases carry interpretive significance — is applied here: since 'a jealous God' (Deuteronomy 5:9) immediately follows 'you shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain' (Deuteronomy 5:11), the juxtaposition teaches a legal equivalence. The Talmud states that one who swears falsely violates the prohibition of 'you shall not take,' and further notes that Scripture links false oaths to idolatry, for one who swears falsely acknowledges two authorities (Shavuot 29a).", :sources ["Shavuot 29a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (סמיכות) of adjacent phrases 'Jealous' and 'You shall not take'", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:30.289460172Z", :model "glm-5.2"}